/**
 * @page guides-ordering Matrix storage and finite-element component ordering
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * Matrix storage, tensor storage and finite-element coefficient numbering
 * describe different representations. Storage order determines the position
 * of an entry in an object's memory. Component numbering determines the
 * association between a coefficient and a basis function. Mathematical
 * indexing remains independent of both representations.
 *
 * @section ordering-matrix Matrix storage
 *
 * @ref Rodin::Math::Matrix and @ref Rodin::Math::FixedSizeMatrix use Eigen's
 * default column-major storage. For a dense matrix with @f$m@f$ rows, entry
 * @f$A_{ij}@f$ has offset @f$i+mj@f$, with zero-based indices. Rows vary
 * fastest within each column. @ref Rodin::Math::SparseMatrix explicitly uses
 * column-major compressed sparse storage; its entries are not a contiguous
 * dense array.
 *
 * @ref Rodin::Math::SpatialMatrix also uses column-major storage, in a fixed
 * three-by-three backing matrix. Its runtime dimensions select an active
 * upper-left block. Consequently, its backing-storage offset is
 * @f$i+3j@f$, including when the active row count is smaller than three.
 * The active entries of a rectangular spatial matrix must not be treated
 * as a packed array with leading dimension equal to its runtime row count.
 * Access through @c operator()(i,j) preserves the mathematical indexing.
 *
 * Explicit row-major Eigen buffers occur in local integration kernels.
 * These are internal storage choices and do not change the default storage
 * of public matrix types or the numbering of finite-element coefficients.
 *
 * @section ordering-components Matrix finite-element coefficients
 *
 * The matrix specializations of P0, P0g, P1 and H1 currently interleave
 * row-major components within each scalar basis index. For an
 * @f$m\times n@f$ range, scalar basis index @f$a@f$ and matrix component
 * @f$(i,j)@f$, the coefficient index is
 * @f[
 *   q=a(mn)+c,\qquad c=in+j.
 * @f]
 * The corresponding basis function is @f$\phi_a E_{ij}@f$, where
 * @f$E_{ij}@f$ is the matrix unit with a single nonzero entry. For a
 * two-by-three range, the component sequence at each scalar basis index is
 * @f[
 *   (0,0),\ (0,1),\ (0,2),\ (1,0),\ (1,1),\ (1,2).
 * @f]
 * This numbering is distinct from column-major matrix storage. No Voigt
 * scaling, symmetry assumption or component reduction is applied. A
 * column-major component convention would instead use @f$c=i+mj@f$;
 * that is not the current matrix-space coefficient convention.
 *
 * Within these space families, local and global component expansion follows
 * this rule relative to their respective scalar DOF maps. Generic algorithms
 * must obtain local-to-global indices from the finite-element space rather
 * than reconstructing them from this formula. Other conforming families,
 * including future @f$H(\mathrm{div})@f$ and @f$H(\mathrm{curl})@f$ spaces,
 * may have different basis functions, mappings and DOF distributions.
 *
 * @section ordering-tensor Spatial tensor storage and index meaning
 *
 * @ref Rodin::Math::SpatialTensor uses a fixed-capacity stack array with
 * runtime extents. Active entries occupy a contiguous prefix, with the last
 * index varying fastest. For rank @f$R@f$, extents @f$d_0,\ldots,d_{R-1}@f$
 * and indices @f$i_0,\ldots,i_{R-1}@f$, the storage offset is
 * @f[
 *   p=\sum_{r=0}^{R-1} i_r\prod_{s=r+1}^{R-1}d_s.
 * @f]
 * The empty product equals one. An extent of zero makes the active tensor
 * empty. This is the tensor analogue of row-major storage and differs from
 * the column-major backing storage of @ref Rodin::Math::SpatialMatrix.
 *
 * Index roles are specified by each mathematical operation. In particular,
 * the gradient of a matrix field has entries
 * @f$G_{ijk}=\partial_k A_{ij}@f$, and a constitutive tensor acts through
 * @f$(\mathcal C A)_{ij}=\sum_{kl}\mathcal C_{ijkl}A_{kl}@f$.
 * These definitions do not depend on storage order. Tensor storage does not
 * define a tensor-valued finite-element DOF convention; the current matrix
 * solution API does not introduce higher-rank tensor solution spaces.
 *
 * @section ordering-assembly Consequences for assembly
 *
 * A change of component numbering permutes coefficient vectors and the rows
 * and columns of assembled operators. Such a change requires coordinated
 * updates to basis evaluation, component access, DOF maps, persistence and
 * interoperability. Changing memory order alone does not redefine components.
 *
 * For uncoupled matrix mass and stiffness forms in the componentwise spaces,
 * interleaved numbering gives @f$A_h=A_{\mathrm{scalar}}\otimes I_{mn}@f$.
 * With @f$N@f$ scalar local basis functions, full dense traversal examines
 * @f$(mn)^2N^2@f$ entries although only @f$mnN^2@f$ component pairs can
 * contribute. Switching between row-major and column-major component
 * numbering does not remove this structural work. Assembly must preserve
 * the form's component coupling to avoid traversing absent pairs; coupled
 * constitutive forms require their actual coupling pattern.
 *
 * @see @ref guides-spatial-matrix "Matrix solutions and spatial tensors"
 * @see @ref fem-spaces "Finite element spaces"
 */
